NumPy (Numerical Python)
A library for fast numerical computing in Python,
built around the ndarray (n-dimensional array) object.
 It's designed for mathematical and scientific operations on numbers
 such as vectors, matrices, and higher-dimensional data
 It structure must be Homogeneous
 which means all elements must be the same type (all int, all float, etc.)
 It indexing is Positional only (integer-based)
 It dimension is any number (1D, 2D, 3D, ...)
 examples
 import numpy as np

arr = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])
arr * 2          #multiply 2 on every element at once
arr.mean()        # finds mean value. mean and sum are aggregation
arr.sum()         #finds total summation of element
np.random.randn(5)   # 5 random numbers from a normal distribution

NumPy is used for:

Mathematical/scientific computing — linear algebra, statistics, simulations
Image processing (an image is just a 2D/3D array of pixel values)
Backend of machine learning — feeding numeric arrays into models (Scikit-learn, TensorFlow, PyTorch all use NumPy arrays internally)
Generating random data for simulations (np.random.normal(), np.random.poisson())
Fast numerical operations on large datasets without loops
______________________________________________________
*
*   Pandas
*_____________________________________________________

Pandas
A library for working with labeled, tabular data in
rows and columns, like a spreadsheet or database table.
Built on top of NumPy, but adds structure: column names,
row indices, mixed data types per column.

It object is either DataFrame (2D table) or Series (1D column)
it structure is Heterogeneous which means each column can be a different type
It indexing are Labeled ( custom row/column names, plus positional)
It dimension are Primarily 1D (Series) and 2D (DataFrame)


Pandas has two core data structures, and they map directly to dimensions:

1D — Series
A Series is a single column of data — like one column from a spreadsheet, with an index attached to each value.
Eg.
import pandas as pd
ages = pd.Series([25, 30, 35, 40], index=['Alice', 'Bob', 'Charlie', 'Dave'])
print(ages)


2D — DataFrame
A DataFrame is a full table with multiple columns or (multiple Series)
sharing the same row index. This is what you get from pd.read_csv()
Eg.
df = pd.DataFrame({
    'name': ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Charlie', 'Dave'],
    'age': [25, 30, 35, 40],
    'city': ['Lagos', 'Abuja', 'Lagos', 'Kano'],
    'score': [88.5, 92.1, 79.4, 95.0]
})
print(df)
example
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd

# NumPy array — all same type
arr = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4])

# Pandas DataFrame — mixed types, labeled columns
df = pd.DataFrame({
    'name': ['Alice', 'Bob'],
    'age': [25, 30],
    'score': [88.5, 92.1]
})

Raw file (CSV/Excel)
      ↓
   Pandas          ← load, clean, filter, group
      ↓
   NumPy           ← Pandas uses NumPy under the hood for the actual math
      ↓
Matplotlib/Seaborn/Plotly   ← visualize the result


example 1
You work as a data analyst for a mid-sized retail chain.
Leadership wants to understand sales performance across
regions and product categories over the past year,
identify underperforming areas, and spot seasonal trends
 before the next quarterly planning meeting.
 --------code ------------
 #Step 1 — Load and Inspect the Data
 import pandas as pd
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import seaborn as sns

# In practice: df = pd.read_csv('sales_data.csv')
# Simulated dataset for illustration
np.random.seed(42)
dates = pd.date_range('2025-01-01', '2025-12-31', freq='D')
regions = ['North', 'South', 'East', 'West']
categories = ['Electronics', 'Clothing', 'Groceries', 'Home & Garden']

df = pd.DataFrame({
    'date': np.random.choice(dates, 5000),
    'region': np.random.choice(regions, 5000),
    'category': np.random.choice(categories, 5000),
    'units_sold': np.random.poisson(8, 5000),
    'unit_price': np.round(np.random.uniform(5, 200, 5000), 2)
})
df['revenue'] = df['units_sold'] * df['unit_price']
df['month'] = df['date'].dt.to_period('M').astype(str)

print(df.head())
print(df.describe())

Step 2 — Monthly Revenue Trend (Line Chart)
monthly_revenue = df.groupby('month')['revenue'].sum().reset_index()

plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))
sns.lineplot(data=monthly_revenue, x='month', y='revenue', marker='o', color='#1f77b4')
plt.xticks(rotation=45)
plt.title('Monthly Revenue Trend — 2025')
plt.ylabel('Revenue ($)')
plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

region_revenue = df.groupby('region')['revenue'].sum().sort_values(ascending=False)

plt.figure(figsize=(7, 5))
sns.barplot(x=region_revenue.index, y=region_revenue.values, palette='viridis')
plt.title('Total Revenue by Region')
plt.ylabel('Revenue ($)')
plt.show()

Step 4 — Category Performance Across Regions (Heatmap)
pivot = df.pivot_table(values='revenue', index='category', columns='region', aggfunc='sum')

plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))
sns.heatmap(pivot, annot=True, fmt='.0f', cmap='YlOrRd')
plt.title('Revenue Heatmap: Category vs Region')
plt.show()

Step 5 — Distribution and Outliers (Box Plot)
plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))
sns.boxplot(data=df, x='category', y='revenue', palette='Set2')
plt.title('Revenue Distribution by Category (Outlier Detection)')
plt.xticks(rotation=15)
plt.show()

# Step 6 Build an Interactive Dashboard Chart (Plotly)
import plotly.express as px

monthly_region = df.groupby(['month', 'region'])['revenue'].sum().reset_index()

fig = px.line(
    monthly_region, x='month', y='revenue', color='region',
    title='Interactive Monthly Revenue by Region',
    markers=True
)
fig.update_layout(xaxis_tickangle=-45)
fig.write_html('sales_dashboard.html')  # can be emailed or embedded in an internal portal
fig.show()